1463 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3584 0 R
/ActualText (Importance of Volume in Breakouts: A breakout occurs when the price moves beyond a key level of support or resistance, signaling a potential new trend. However, not all breakouts are created equal. A breakout accompanied by high volume is much more likely to be genuine and sustained than one with low volume. I go into more detail on breakouts versus fakeouts later in the book.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1464 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3585 0 R
/ActualText (Analogy: Consider a dam breaking. A small crack \(low volume\) might not be enough to cause a significant breach, but a large burst of water \(high volume\) will likely lead to a dramatic and irreversible change. Similarly, a breakout with high volume suggests strong market participation and increases the likelihood of the price continuing in the new direction.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1465 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3586 0 R
/ActualText (Practical Example: If a stock breaks above a key resistance level and you see a significant increase in volume at the same time, it indicates that the breakout is supported by strong buying interest, making it more likely to hold.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
