/S /H1
/T (The Psychology Behind Support and Resistance Levels)
/E (The Psychology Behind Support and Resistance Levels)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1489 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Understanding the psychology driving support and resistance is crucial for any trader. These levels aren't just lines on a chart; they represent the collective mindset of market participants.)
/E (Understanding the psychology driving support and resistance is crucial for any trader. These levels aren't just lines on a chart; they represent the collective mindset of market participants.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1490 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3594 0 R
/ActualText (Memory and Anchoring: Traders and investors tend to remember significant price points. If a stock previously struggled to break above $100, for instance, that price becomes anchored in traders' minds. When the price approaches that level again, many will expect it to act as resistance, often creating a self-fulfilling prophecy.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1491 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3595 0 R
/ActualText (Round Numbers: Psychologically, we're drawn to round numbers. Levels like $50, $100, or $1000 often act as support or resistance simply because many traders place their orders at these "psychologically significant" prices.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1492 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3596 0 R
/ActualText (Pain and Regret: Support often forms at levels where traders who missed out on a previous rally wish they had bought. If the price returns to that level, they see it as a second chance and buy, creating support. Conversely, resistance can form where traders who bought at a peak and saw the price decline wish they had sold. If the price returns to that level, they often sell to break even, creating resistance.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
1493 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Ki2)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
