/S /P
/P 3601 0 R
/ActualText (Timeframe: The falling wedge can develop over different timeframes, from short-term intraday charts to long-term weekly or monthly charts. The principles of identification remain the same, but the implications of the pattern might vary depending on the timeframe. A falling wedge on a longer timeframe often signals a more significant potential reversal.)
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1553 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3602 0 R
/ActualText (Pattern Length: The pattern can last from a few days to several weeks or even months. The length of time the pattern takes to form can influence the strength of the subsequent breakout. Generally, the longer the wedge forms, the more powerful the breakout is likely to be.)
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1554 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3603 0 R
/ActualText (Breakout Direction: While a falling wedge is typically a bullish pattern, it's essential to wait for the actual breakout before making any trading decisions. The breakout should occur above the upper trendline, and ideally, it should be accompanied by an increase in volume. This confirms that the pattern has completed and the bullish reversal is in play.)
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1555 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3604 0 R
/ActualText (Volume Patterns: Besides decreasing volume as the wedge forms, watch for a volume spike at the point of breakout. This spike is a strong indicator that the market is ready to reverse, as it shows that new buyers are stepping in with conviction.)
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
