/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3993 0 R
/ActualText (Confirmation Bias: This bias occurs when you seek out information that supports your existing beliefs while ignoring data that contradicts them. For example, if you believe a stock is going to rise, you might only focus on bullish signals and disregard bearish indicators, leading to skewed decision-making.)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
2541 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3994 0 R
/ActualText (Recency Bias: This bias involves giving undue weight to recent events when making decisions. For instance, if a stock has been climbing steadily, you might assume that it will continue to do so, even if broader market conditions suggest otherwise. Recency bias can lead to overconfidence in the continuation of trends or undue pessimism following recent losses.)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
2542 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Emotional Influence on Perception)
/E (Emotional Influence on Perception)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
2543 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Emotions such as fear, greed, and hope can heavily influence how you interpret market information. Fear may cause you to see danger where none exists, leading to missed opportunities or premature exits. Greed can make you blind to risks, encouraging you to hold onto positions longer than you should or to take on trades that exceed your risk tolerance. Maintaining emotional neutrality is crucial for objective analysis. By recognizing how emotions color your perception, you can take steps to mitigate their impact on your trading decisions.)
/E (Emotions such as fear, greed, and hope can heavily influence how you interpret market information. Fear may cause you to see danger where none exists, leading to missed opportunities or premature exits. Greed can make you blind to risks, encouraging you to hold onto positions longer than you should or to take on trades that exceed your risk tolerance. Maintaining emotional neutrality is crucial for objective analysis. By recognizing how emotions color your perception, you can take steps to mitigate their impact on your trading decisions.)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [11]
>>
